# Universe content · life · plants (domain Eukaryotes, kingdom Plantae)
# researched: land-plant phylogeny summaries (Wikipedia "Embryophyte", Macmillan biology ch. 33);
#   plant kingdom overviews; standard botany for gymnosperm lines, monocots and eudicots, organs,
#   vascular tissue, C3/C4/CAM photosynthesis and alternation of generations
# Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md · wiki/systems/r08-life-sim.md

# ── Groups (phyla and lines) ──

@group liverworts
name: Liverworts
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: flat, leafy, damp-ground plants with no veins; among the first on land

@group mosses
name: Mosses
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: soft carpets with no veins; need water to breed

@group hornworts
name: Hornworts
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: small plants with horn-shaped spore stalks

@group lycophytes
name: Club mosses and spike mosses
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: ancient spore plants with veins; once giant trees in coal-age swamps

@group ferns-horsetails
name: Ferns and horsetails
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: spore plants with veins and big fronds or jointed stems

@group cycads
name: Cycads
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: palm-like seed plants with cones

@group ginkgo
name: Ginkgo
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: one living species left, a living fossil tree

@group conifers
name: Conifers
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: pines, firs, spruces, cedars and redwoods; needles and cones

@group gnetophytes
name: Gnetophytes
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: a small, strange group of seed plants

@group flowering-plants
name: Flowering plants
real: yes
kingdom: plants
says: by far the biggest group, over 380,000 species: flowers, enclosed seeds, fruit
kinds: monocots (grasses, palms, orchids, lilies), eudicots (most flowers, trees and crops)

# ── Big splits ──

@split non-vascular
name: Plants without veins
real: yes
says: mosses, liverworts, hornworts; small, close to the ground, need damp

@split vascular
name: Plants with veins
real: yes
says: tubes carry water up and food down, so they can grow tall

@split spore-plants
name: Spore plants
real: yes
says: breed with spores, not seeds

@split naked-seed
name: Naked-seed plants
real: yes
says: gymnosperms: seeds on cones, not inside fruit

@split enclosed-seed
name: Enclosed-seed plants
real: yes
says: flowering plants: seeds grow inside fruit

# ── Organs and tissues ──

@part roots
name: Roots
real: yes
says: anchor the plant and drink water and minerals

@part rhizoids
name: Rhizoids
real: yes
says: simple root-like threads on mosses and liverworts

@part stem
name: Stem
real: yes
says: holds the plant up and carries water and food

@part leaves
name: Leaves
real: yes
says: catch starlight and make food

@part flower
name: Flower
real: yes
says: the breeding part, often built to lure pollinators

@part seed
name: Seed
real: yes
says: a baby plant packed with food in a tough coat

@part fruit
name: Fruit
real: yes
says: wraps the seeds, often to be eaten and carried away by animals

@part xylem
name: Xylem
real: yes
says: tubes carrying water up from the roots; in trees it becomes wood

@part phloem
name: Phloem
real: yes
says: tubes carrying food from the leaves to the rest of the plant

@part bark
name: Bark
real: yes
says: the protective skin of woody stems

@part thorns
name: Thorns and spines
real: yes
says: defense against eaters

# ── How they make food ──

@photosynthesis c3
name: Standard photosynthesis (C3)
real: yes
says: the most common way; works best in mild, damp places

@photosynthesis c4
name: Hot-climate photosynthesis (C4)
real: yes
says: concentrates carbon dioxide to work in heat and bright light
example: corn, sugarcane, many grasses

@photosynthesis cam
name: Desert photosynthesis (CAM)
real: yes
says: opens its pores only at night to save water
example: cacti, agave, pineapple

@photosynthesis parasitic
name: Plant parasites
real: yes
says: some plants steal food from other plants instead of making their own

@photosynthesis carnivorous
name: Carnivorous plants
real: yes
says: trap insects for extra nutrients
example: Venus flytrap, pitcher plants

# ── How they breed ──

@breed alternation
name: Alternation of generations
real: yes
says: plants swap between a spore-making form and a sex-cell-making form each generation

@breed spores
name: Spores
real: yes

@breed pollen
name: Pollen
real: yes
says: carried by wind, insects, birds or bats

@breed seeds
name: Seeds
real: yes

@breed runners
name: Runners and cloning
real: yes
says: copies of themselves from roots, runners or broken pieces

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule plant-land
real: rule
says: plants climb onto land from green algae, with root-partner fungi helping; veins let them grow tall; seeds free them from water

@rule plant-climate
real: rule
says: climate decides the kind: C3 in mild damp places, C4 in hot grasslands, CAM in deserts

@rule plant-forests
real: rule
says: forests make soil and air, and when buried they become coal

@rule plant-partners
real: rule
says: flowering plants and pollinators evolve together; fruit evolves with the animals that spread it

@rule plant-defense
real: rule
says: plants fight back with thorns, poisons, bark and chemical warnings to their neighbors

@rule plant-farming
real: rule
says: thinking species farm, breed and engineer plants (Farming, Selective Breeding, Genetic Engineering)
